-- title:
--   Places over a centre from the roots of Φ_N
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, $N$ a nonzero natural number with $q \nmid N$, and $K$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q$. Let `data` supply a monic $\Phi \in (\mathbb{Z}[X])[Y]$ of degree $\psi(N) = \sum_{d \mid N,\ d \text{ squarefree}} N/d$ with $\Phi$ vanishing at the $q$-expansions of $j$ and $j_N$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Fix $a \in K$, a Laurent series $x_0$ over $K$, and a subgroup $\Gamma \le K^\times$ such that each $u \in \Gamma$ fixes $x_0$ under `qTwist` $u$ (the substitution $X \mapsto uX$, multiplying the $k$-th coefficient by $u^k$), with $\mathrm{Nat.card}\,\Gamma$ nonzero in $K$ and equal to the order of $x_0 - a$. Assume $\Phi(x_0, Y)$, the polynomial obtained by evaluating each coefficient of $\Phi$ at $x_0$, has exactly $\psi(N)$ distinct roots in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,K$. Let $F = K(\bar{j}, \bar{j}_N)$ be the intermediate field of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,K$ generated by `jqModC` $K$ and `jqNModC` $K$ $N$, and let $T$ be a finite set of places of $F$ over $K$ (valuation subrings, proper, principal, containing $K$) consisting exactly of those $w$ with $\mathrm{ord}_w(\bar{j} - a) > 0$. Then there is a map $f$ from the roots $y$ of $\Phi(x_0, Y)$ to places of $F$ over $K$ such that: for each $y$ there is a $K$-algebra embedding $\iota : F \to \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,K$ with $\iota(\bar{j}) = x_0$, $\iota(\bar{j}_N) = y$, whose valuation subring of nonnegative-order elements is that of $f(y)$; every $w \in T$ lies in the image of $f$; $f(y) = f(y')$ holds precisely when $y' =$ `qTwist` $u$ $y$ for some $u \in \Gamma$; and for each $y$ the number of roots in the $\Gamma$-orbit of $y$ equals $\mathrm{ord}_{f(y)}(\bar{j} - a)$.
--
--   This is the local dictionary at a centre $a$ of the $j$-line in characteristic $q \nmid N$: places of the geometric modular function field of level $N$ lying over $\bar{j} = a$ are parametrised by $\Gamma$-orbits of the roots of the modular polynomial specialised at $x_0$, the orbit size being the ramification index. It is used in the comparison of the supersingular places of level $N$ with the fibre over a generic centre.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_map_roots_places_of_card_roots_eq_dedekindPsi_univ.lean

